{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792329432,"nonce":"i/aorNPYnASmUabLDl3Cykj5vUI=","title":"wasm collections are all full and slow, and they keep unreachable data alive","message":"The user's report on ashui's browser demo: it freezes sometimes while it is moved, and the tab is a gigabyte. Two causes in the collector, with numbers from `ASH_GC_STATS=1` passed to the page as an environment variable.\n\n**Full collections of a big heap**\n\nNothing on wasm is generational (the write barrier is page protection, unix only), so every collection is a full one. With the demo's heap at 230 MB live, mostly pointer-free bytes, each pauses 130 to 280 ms, every four or five seconds while it is used:\n\n    [gc] #7  t=3.69s   pause=131ms  live=6205 blocks (193.9MB)\n    [gc] #8  t=7.49s   pause=173ms  live=7091 blocks (221.6MB)\n    [gc] #13 t=30.98s  pause=276ms  live=7485 blocks (233.9MB)\n\nThe same scene on the desktop has the same live set, 156 MB, and pauses of 2 to 9 ms, as minor collections. The mark time is the cost, 125 ms of 131; a heap that is nearly all noscan blocks should not cost that to mark.\n\n**Unreachable data stays live**\n\nIn the wasm build, with a forced `hl.Gc.major()` at each step: 7.9 MB live before the first frame, 188 MB after it, and it stays: 190.9 MB at frame 5, 196 MB at frame 120. One canvas (a street map's tessellation, 381 000 vertices: a 21 MB float array, an 18.6 MB byte buffer, 1486 cached shapes) is 98 MB of it. The program drops every reference to that canvas's buffers two seconds after building it, and the next collections free 1, 6 and 21 blocks and the live size does not move (184.4 MB before, 184.1 MB after). Natively the same program, with the same references dropped, loses the 17.6 MB buffer from the census and its pointer-free live bytes go from 126 MB to 97 MB.\n\nSo the wasm collector keeps dead data reachable. The first frame is the deepest the program's stack goes; if the roots are the whole fiber stack or the whole shadow stack, and not the part below the stack pointer, stale pointers left by that frame would keep everything those buffers refer to alive until something overwrites them.\n\n**Wanted**\n\nThe roots of a wasm thread scanned from the stack pointer up, nothing below it. A look at why mark is slow on mostly noscan blocks. And, for pauses, whatever is cheapest for wasm: incremental marking, or a generational scheme that does not need page protection (a software card table).","files":null}]}